\relax 
\providecommand\zref@newlabel[2]{}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces An image of a tomographic model of S-wave velocity variations within the Earth produced by John Woodhouse and Adam Dziewonski. The colours show the perturbation in S-wave velocity relative to a spherical reference model, with blue areas being faster than normal, and red areas slower. Areas of faster velocity are thought to be colder than normal, while those that are slower are hotter, and hence the chosen colour scale. These temperature anomalies have associated changes in density, and so the models tell us something about the buoyancy forces that drive mantle convection.\relax }}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:1}{{1}{2}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces An example of a simple two-dimensional tomographic problem. There are a number of ``earthquakes'' and ``seismometers'', and the straight lines denote ray paths. The colours plotted denote the velocity perturbation. On the left is the input model, and on the right the inverted model obtained using a certain technique. In regions where there is no ray coverage, the recovered model sets the velocity to zero; this gives a clear example of what is meant by the null space.\relax }}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:2}{{2}{6}}
